meta {
  name: Stream all events
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/events/workflows
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Accept: text/event-stream
}

docs {
  Server-Sent Events stream of every WorkflowEvent emitted by the daemon.

  Event types (each delivered as `data: <json>` over SSE):
  - `RunStarted` — { run_id, workflow_id, workflow_version, started_at }
  - `StepStarted` — { run_id, workflow_id, step_index, step_id, kind, started_at }
  - `StepOutput` — { run_id, workflow_id, step_index, step_id, data, timestamp } (chunk of stdout/stderr)
  - `StepCompleted` — { run_id, workflow_id, step_index, step_id, exit_code, cost_usd, finished_at }
  - `RunCompleted` — { run_id, workflow_id, status, total_cost_usd, finished_at }
  - `RunFailed` — { run_id, workflow_id, error, finished_at }
  - `WorkflowChanged` — { workflow_id, version, change_kind } (`created` / `updated` / `deleted` / `enabled` / `disabled`)

  Each event is dispatched with a named SSE `event:` line in addition to the JSON `data:` payload. The names are snake_case: `run_started`, `step_started`, `step_output`, `step_completed`, `run_completed`, `run_failed`, `workflow_changed`.

  The server emits a 15-second SSE keep-alive comment (`:keepalive`). When the broadcast channel lags, it emits `: lagged: some workflow events were missed`. There is no server-side reconnect; clients use standard EventSource auto-reconnect.

  Bruno's HTTP runner displays incoming SSE events live. Open this request,
  hit Send, then trigger a workflow in another tab — you'll see the run
  unfold in real time.
}
